@using MartenStudio.Services

@*
    Why the mutating controls beside this are disabled, when the reason is the visitor rather than the
    application.

    It is a sentence and not a tooltip because a tooltip does not exist on a touch screen, and this is the
    one refusal a person cannot work out from the screen on their own: the buttons are there, the studio
    plainly supports the operation, and nothing they can change will make them work. The control stays
    rendered and disabled rather than hidden - "this studio can do this and your account may not" is a
    different fact from "this studio cannot do this", and somebody who cannot tell the two apart goes and
    asks the wrong person for the wrong thing.

    Nothing is said where the capability itself is off. That case is about the application rather than the
    person, names the exact option a host would set, and has <CapabilityDisabled /> for it.

    None of this is the enforcement: every mutating service resolves the scope with the same capability and
    throws regardless of what was rendered (AGENTS.md hard rule 5).
*@

@if (Refused)
{
    <span class="ms-write-refusal" role="note">@WritePolicyRefusal.For(Capability)</span>
}

@code {
    /// <summary>
    /// Whether <see cref="MartenStudioOptions.WriteAuthorizationPolicy" /> refused this visitor.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// The page asks once per load and hands the answer down. A component that asked for itself would put
    /// an <c>await</c> in a render path and would ask again for every row.
    /// </remarks>
    [Parameter]
    public bool Refused { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// The capability that was refused, by name.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// A string rather than the internal <c>StudioCapability</c> enum: the Razor compiler emits a public
    /// component class, and a public parameter cannot have an internal type. Call sites pass
    /// <c>nameof(StudioCapability.ArchiveStreams)</c>, which the compiler still checks.
    /// </remarks>
    [Parameter]
    [EditorRequired]
    public string Capability { get; set; } = string.Empty;
}
